1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Remember we did two different versions of probability.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Objective probability, which you don't know anyone who takes a really hard line and just says,

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
I refuse to accept that could be a thing.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,000
And then subjective probability, where there are some people saying, no, no, no, there's something wrong here.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
You can't say these things. You can't say that you have a 40, that you think there's a 40% chance of something happening.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
I don't know if I just said it. There's something, some people think there's something illegitimate.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
But anyway, all of modern economics is based upon saying that it's perfectly fine to use subjective probability.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
So we're going to do that. And if you do have some philosophical objection, you just have to suspend disbelief and work with it anyway.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
You may be amazed at how far you can get talking about unicorns, even though there are no unicorns.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Now one interesting thing about subjective probability is that it doesn't put any constraints at all on what you can believe before you see facts.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
You believe you can have any probabilities you want. 99% probability that I am the emperor of Antarctica.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Which is if you have John Nash out, you don't have to be the emperor of the world.

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
But it does actually restrict the way that you can update these beliefs.

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:25,000
So built in to subjective probability are some rules about how updating or revising beliefs in response to what you see has to change.

15
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
And there's two big formulas that are worth knowing.

16
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
First one is just the conditional probability formula.

17
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Which most of you probably either know or can just intuit.

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
And this just says the probability of A given B.

19
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And probability, whenever you see that vertical line, that means given.

20
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
So the probability of A given that B has occurred or that B is seen or whatever.

21
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Is going to be equal to the probability that you get both A and B divided by the probability of B.

22
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
So a classic example would be what's the probability that you get two coins that both flip heads?

23
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Conditional upon or given that the first one already flipped heads.

24
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
And then, well, the probability that you get two coins flipping heads is going to be 0.25.

25
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
It's a fair coin, right?

26
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
You flip it twice, you probably get heads twice in a row.

27
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
It's going to be 1 in 4.

28
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Probability that the B happened is 0.5.

29
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Then you go through and then you come up with probability of 0.5.

30
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:40,000
So this means that there's a 1 in 4 probability that you would get two heads before you know what the first one was.

31
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
But then the second one, before you flipped it, you already have.

32
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
But before you flipped it, you've not had a 50% chance.

33
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
So the probability that you will get two heads given you've already got one head is going to be 0.5.

34
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,000
On the other hand, you say what's the probability that you will get two heads given the first one was a tails?

35
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Of course, that's impossible, right?

36
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Now that is just a classic boring example.

37
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
We could also use this for a more interesting probability problem.

38
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Like suppose a child claims that he saw a monster.

39
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
A monster! I've seen a monster!

40
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
And then, if you were to apply this formula, you start with, alright,

41
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
well, there's the probability the child saw a monster and there really was a monster.

42
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
So, I'm going to turn out what counts as a monster.

43
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
You know, like a Tyrannosaurus, that would count.

44
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
You know, just a list of things that would be good contenders.

45
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
I think if it turned out there was a Tyrannosaurus Rex in the backyard,

46
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,000
I think you would be very pedantic to say, well, a dinosaur is not a true monster.

47
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
But, in any case, so just picture this.

48
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Alright, so the probability he saw a monster and there was a monster,

49
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
divided by the probability he says he saw a monster.

50
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
So, if the probability he says he saw a monster and there was a monster is one in a billion,

51
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
a very low probability,

52
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
and the probability that he says he saw a monster is, say, 0.1,

53
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
10% chance a kid will go and just make up any kind of outlandish story.

54
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
But, if you're my younger son, it's more like a 90% chance it's going to be an outlandish story.

55
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Then the conditional probability turns out to be more than 100 million.

56
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
So, even though, of course, you still think it's highly unlikely there's a monster,

57
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
the fact the kid said it does dramatically multiply the probability,

58
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
by a factor of 10.

59
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
And, of course, that's more than it's only small probability to begin with,

60
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
and it's not very impressive.

61
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
This is actually a general point about probability.

62
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
You'll notice in the media, sometimes they'll say that there's a 47% increase

63
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
in the number of puppies that Sikhs have killed in the last month.

64
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Alright, you're like, wait a second, how long does it happen anyway?

65
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
So, well, there were two incidents last month and three this month,

66
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
so roughly a 47% increase, something like that.

67
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
So, in general, whenever you see the media going and switching to percent changes,

68
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
it is a reason to think that maybe it's an enormous change over a very small base,

69
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
and is therefore still absolutely nothing a sane person would ever worry about or talk about

70
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
or pay attention to.

71
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
But, that's your audience, so I can make it in advertising, so don't worry about it.

72
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
And then a general point, a conjunction can never be more probable than either of the components.

73
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
So, a classic example here of probability, or actually in experimental psychology,

74
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
so there's a famous experiment where you randomly assign people to two groups,

75
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
so, randomly assign the even number of people to one group, odd number of people to another group,

76
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
I split you up so you don't find out what the other one is hearing,

77
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:34,000
and I go to both groups, and I give you the same story about, I don't know what her name is,

78
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
it's like a woman named Carol or something like that.

79
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
So, both of you here, you know, Carol is a long-time environmentalist.

80
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
She has always been concerned with animals and flowers.

81
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:51,000
When she was an undergraduate, she majored in environmental science, something like that.

82
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
But then, you both hear the same prompt, but then group one is told,

83
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
what's the probability that Carol is a banker?

84
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
And group two is asked, what's the probability that Carol is a banker who is active in environmental causes?

85
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
And, bizarrely, the people who were just asked, probably she's a banker,

86
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
were like, no, no way it's Carol a banker.

87
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
But the people who were asked if she's a banker active in environmental causes,

88
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
were like, no, no, 20%.

89
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
It's like 1% says she's a banker, 20% she's a banker active in environmental causes,

90
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
which, basic probability is absolutely absurd, that can't be.

91
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Because, the worst case scenario is that if she's a banker, she's always active in environmental causes.

92
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
I'm not even, I'm not involved in that one.

93
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:43,000
In which case, if there's a 20% probability that she's a banker active in environmental causes,

94
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
the probability that she's a banker of some sort or other, must be greater than or equal to 20%, right?

95
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
So, this is a big issue in psychology.

96
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Alright, now there's another formula, this one is one where a lot of people can kind of figure it out,

97
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
you don't need to know the formula to go do problems like this,

98
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
maybe a hard one you might need the formula.

99
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:09,000
There's another one that virtually no one can do without the formula, and this is called Bayes' Rule.

100
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Alright, and this links the probability of A given B to the probability of B given A.

101
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:22,000
So what this says is that the probability of A given B is equal to the probability of B given A,

102
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:28,000
times the probability of A, all over the probability of B given A,

103
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:35,000
times the probability of A, plus the probability of B given not A,

104
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:41,000
the tilde means not in probability of mutation, times the probability of not A.

105
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Now I'm sure this is instantly obvious to all of you,

106
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,000
and you never, you know, if you're the age of five you've been going around reciting this formula.

107
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
But anyway, this is the formula, right, and you have one example,

108
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
so I'm not going to prove it, but you want to look at one example of this.

109
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
So, you know, we just go and plug in for A and B,

110
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:03,000
that says the probability that a child saw a monster, given that he says he saw a monster,

111
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
is equal to the probability that he says he saw a monster if he did see a monster,

112
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
times probability of saw a monster, all divided by the probability saw a monster,

113
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:15,000
times the probability that he, you know, times the probability that he says he saw a monster if he saw a monster,

114
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
plus the probability that he say saw a monster if did not see a monster,

115
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
times the probability that he did not see a monster.

116
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:24,000
So, for example, if the probability that he says he saw a monster, if he did see a monster is one,

117
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,360
So if he sees a monster, he definitely can tell you.

118
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:29,240
And the probability he says he saw a monster

119
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:30,740
and he did not see a monster is 0.1.

120
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:33,120
So 10% chance the crowd is making an outrageous lie

121
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:34,640
to make a tension.

122
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:37,160
And the probability he saw a monster is, say, 1 in 10

123
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:37,880
million.

124
00:08:37,880 --> 00:08:39,720
And the probability he didn't see a monster

125
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:42,600
is going to be, of course, 1 minus 1 in 10 million.

126
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:44,880
Then the conditional probability that he did, in fact,

127
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:47,480
see a monster and he says it is going to work out

128
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:53,920
to the 0.9999991 times to the negative 7.

129
00:08:53,920 --> 00:08:57,280
So again, this has raised the probability.

130
00:08:57,280 --> 00:09:00,040
Now, what's interesting about Bayesian's rule?

131
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:01,580
Bayesian's rule actually gives rise

132
00:09:01,580 --> 00:09:05,280
to an entire philosophy of science called Bayesianism.

133
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:06,560
The Bayesian view of science.

134
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:09,960
And in fact, it gives rise to an entire view of epistemology

135
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:13,560
called Bayesian's view of epistemology.

136
00:09:13,560 --> 00:09:16,400
And just thinking about it for science,

137
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:18,480
there's a natural framework for scientists

138
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:20,760
to relate hypotheses to evidence.

139
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:22,680
So there is a lingering idea that you

140
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:24,960
do a crucial experiment, and then this

141
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:26,440
either proves your theory is true

142
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:29,680
or proves your theory is false.

143
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:32,000
Now, has this ever happened in reality, ever,

144
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,080
in the history of science?

145
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:34,920
No.

146
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:36,400
No way.

147
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:37,320
So think about this.

148
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:40,520
Have you ever done an experiment in, say, high school chemistry

149
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:42,000
and you get the wrong answer?

150
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,360
And then the whole scientific community comes, oh my god,

151
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:46,440
a high school in Virginia.

152
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:48,120
The experiment was done disproving

153
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:51,720
the fundamental basis of relativity.

154
00:09:51,720 --> 00:09:53,040
No.

155
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:55,120
So a minimum, if there's some questions

156
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:57,840
about the competence of the experimenter,

157
00:09:57,840 --> 00:09:59,800
then some doubts will be raised.

158
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:01,640
And of course, there's always doubts

159
00:10:01,640 --> 00:10:03,360
about the competence of the experimenter.

160
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:07,800
Even the very best person can screw up.

161
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:09,880
So even if the experiment's been done 10 times,

162
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:12,600
well, wait a second, who conducted this experiment?

163
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:14,360
Maybe there was some bias, or maybe there

164
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:16,160
was something else going on.

165
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:17,640
All these things.

166
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:21,000
So anyway, in general, if you let A be an hypothesis,

167
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,280
so A is an hypothesis of any kind,

168
00:10:24,280 --> 00:10:29,960
and B is some evidence, B is some evidence of any kind,

169
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:31,680
then we can calculate the probability

170
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:35,640
that the hypothesis is true given the evidence.

171
00:10:35,640 --> 00:10:37,880
So for example, the probability that the minimum wage

172
00:10:37,880 --> 00:10:41,040
causes unemployment, given Karn and Kruger's famous study

173
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:43,760
on the minimum wage in New Jersey and Pennsylvania.

174
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:45,960
How many have heard of the famous Karn-Kruger study

175
00:10:45,960 --> 00:10:48,400
in New York, Pennsylvania?

176
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:51,160
So when I was doing labor at Princeton,

177
00:10:51,160 --> 00:10:52,320
I had to present the paper.

178
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:54,320
Yes, I was a Karn student, although I never saw him

179
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:55,560
talk about anything empirical.

180
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:59,160
He only did the theory for a class.

181
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:01,320
But anyway, so a famous study which

182
00:11:01,320 --> 00:11:04,320
seemed to show that when New Jersey raised its minimum wage

183
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:06,640
and Pennsylvania didn't, that New Jersey's low-skilled

184
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:10,120
employment actually increased relative to Pennsylvania.

185
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:12,960
There's a bunch of complaints about the study.

186
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:15,640
Most of them seem to come from people who have not read

187
00:11:15,640 --> 00:11:18,280
the study, because there's a bunch of complaints

188
00:11:18,280 --> 00:11:21,640
where they do specifically address various concerns.

189
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:22,520
Is it a perfect study?

190
00:11:22,520 --> 00:11:23,520
Of course not.

191
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:26,960
But a lot more careful than critics would have you believe.

192
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:29,200
So anyway, suppose that the probability

193
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:31,400
that we get these findings that the minimum wage does

194
00:11:31,400 --> 00:11:35,960
cause unemployment is only going to be 0.3.

195
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:37,560
Right now, why?

196
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:40,640
So if, in fact, the usual view that minimum wage does

197
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:43,840
cause unemployment is true, why would we get these results?

198
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:46,080
Well, maybe our econometric results suck.

199
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:47,480
Maybe econometric methods suck.

200
00:11:47,480 --> 00:11:48,960
Maybe the data sucks.

201
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:51,800
Maybe there's human fallibility.

202
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:53,520
In general, any time someone goes and does

203
00:11:53,520 --> 00:11:55,600
an analysis of some horrible accident,

204
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:59,600
the most likely answer for what went wrong is human error.

205
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:00,520
Why the plane crashed?

206
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:01,920
Human error.

207
00:12:01,920 --> 00:12:02,920
Why was the car crashed?

208
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:03,720
Human error.

209
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:05,160
Why was there a ship crash?

210
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:06,880
Human error.

211
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:08,840
So suppose that you would have a probability

212
00:12:08,840 --> 00:12:10,240
that you would get the findings.

213
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:13,080
Go ahead and get the findings.

214
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:16,760
If minimum wage does cause unemployment,

215
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:18,120
suppose that's 0.3.

216
00:12:18,120 --> 00:12:19,840
So still, it's less likely the chance,

217
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:22,000
but there's still a good chance that the methods just

218
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,720
aren't that good enough to actually give the right answer,

219
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:27,400
even when the theory's true.

220
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:29,400
And on the other hand, suppose the probability

221
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:31,480
that we'll get their findings, so the probability

222
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:33,840
that we'll get the findings if minimum wage does not

223
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:38,680
cause unemployment, does not cause unemployment,

224
00:12:38,680 --> 00:12:41,000
suppose that equals 0.8.

225
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,280
This says that if, in fact, minimum wage doesn't

226
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:46,200
cause unemployment, there's an 80% chance we'll get.

227
00:12:46,200 --> 00:12:47,160
These findings.

228
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:50,000
So that could be a probability.

229
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,120
And then, now we come up with a key thing,

230
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:56,440
which is if you knew nothing about the empirical literature,

231
00:12:56,440 --> 00:12:58,200
and someone says, yeah, what do you

232
00:12:58,200 --> 00:13:00,440
think the odds are that raising the price of asparagus

233
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:03,520
would reduce the amount of asparagus people buy?

234
00:13:03,520 --> 00:13:06,040
Any product, so any random product.

235
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:06,960
What would you say?

236
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:08,520
What would your odds be?

237
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:09,400
I haven't seen any evidence.

