likely to do it.

105
00:23:40,880 --> 00:23:43,720
All right, so now moving into general equilibrium.

106
00:23:43,720 --> 00:23:45,840
Just to let you know, so when I was in your shoes,

107
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:48,000
I could do six weeks on this stuff.

108
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,080
And I've squeezed it down to one week.

109
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:52,760
I think that it deserves one week.

110
00:23:52,760 --> 00:23:54,680
It doesn't deserve any more than one week.

111
00:23:54,680 --> 00:23:56,840
And especially because this is what

112
00:23:56,840 --> 00:23:58,960
we call a mature research literature.

113
00:23:58,960 --> 00:24:01,920
Almost nothing new is being done on general equilibrium.

114
00:24:01,920 --> 00:24:05,560
So it's not very useful to you except just

115
00:24:05,560 --> 00:24:06,800
to understand the background.

116
00:24:06,800 --> 00:24:09,520
And insofar as it does give you some insight in the world,

117
00:24:09,520 --> 00:24:10,320
that's good.

118
00:24:10,320 --> 00:24:12,320
But it's not the kind of thing that it's likely

119
00:24:12,320 --> 00:24:15,280
that anyone here is going to be doing more research on.

120
00:24:15,280 --> 00:24:17,760
Because probably anyone's doing any more research on it.

121
00:24:17,760 --> 00:24:21,000
It's just a very thoroughly fished field.

122
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,480
Although it's possible the next great general equilibrium

123
00:24:23,480 --> 00:24:24,760
theorist is here.

124
00:24:24,760 --> 00:24:26,720
It's possible.

125
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:29,920
All right, so just to back up.

126
00:24:29,920 --> 00:24:32,680
All right, so usually we think about, in economics,

127
00:24:32,680 --> 00:24:35,040
we think about individual agents as maximizing

128
00:24:35,040 --> 00:24:36,040
something or other.

129
00:24:36,040 --> 00:24:40,040
Maximize utility, maximizing profits, maximizing votes.

130
00:24:40,040 --> 00:24:41,720
These are all things that we imagine

131
00:24:41,720 --> 00:24:43,600
that people are maximizing.

132
00:24:43,600 --> 00:24:47,040
But we actually rarely analyze an isolated individual.

133
00:24:47,040 --> 00:24:48,560
So there is crucial economics where

134
00:24:48,560 --> 00:24:51,600
you just analyze Robinson Crusoe on an island.

135
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:55,240
But it's not very interesting in terms of explaining the world.

136
00:24:55,240 --> 00:24:58,760
We don't have a lot of one person societies.

137
00:24:58,760 --> 00:25:00,400
So anyway, for most social scientists,

138
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:04,640
interesting questions involve more than one person.

139
00:25:04,640 --> 00:25:07,360
Interesting questions involve more than one person.

140
00:25:07,360 --> 00:25:08,840
Unfortunately, there's a case where

141
00:25:08,840 --> 00:25:10,800
the interesting questions are really hard.

142
00:25:10,800 --> 00:25:12,200
So we've got a whole body of theory

143
00:25:12,200 --> 00:25:15,720
for analyzing what happens when there is just one individual.

144
00:25:15,720 --> 00:25:18,240
But what about when there are a bunch of individuals

145
00:25:18,240 --> 00:25:20,320
that are all interacting?

146
00:25:20,320 --> 00:25:23,120
So in particular, so you've got one individual doing

147
00:25:23,120 --> 00:25:25,200
what we call playing against nature, one person

148
00:25:25,200 --> 00:25:26,920
on the island.

149
00:25:26,920 --> 00:25:29,280
If that's all that's going on, then the action

150
00:25:29,280 --> 00:25:30,440
is very one-sided.

151
00:25:30,440 --> 00:25:32,720
So for example, Robinson Crusoe shows up on an island

152
00:25:32,720 --> 00:25:34,880
and he starts hunting turtles and fish.

153
00:25:34,880 --> 00:25:37,400
Does anyone think maybe there'll be a turtle fish alliance

154
00:25:37,400 --> 00:25:40,120
to overthrow Crusoe?

155
00:25:40,120 --> 00:25:41,960
Highly unlikely.

156
00:25:41,960 --> 00:25:43,960
It's just not going to happen.

157
00:25:43,960 --> 00:25:45,600
The nature that you're interacting with

158
00:25:45,600 --> 00:25:49,120
is so dumb as a brick, so unstrategic,

159
00:25:49,120 --> 00:25:52,080
that you may as well just figure that there's

160
00:25:52,080 --> 00:25:53,760
no strategy there at all.

161
00:25:53,760 --> 00:25:55,680
I remember once when I was out of high school,

162
00:25:55,680 --> 00:25:57,800
I went hunting crawfish with a net.

163
00:25:57,800 --> 00:26:00,040
And at first, we tried hunting them from the front.

164
00:26:00,040 --> 00:26:02,200
And a crawfish has a distinctive reaction

165
00:26:02,200 --> 00:26:06,240
when it's approached, which it flips its claws this way

166
00:26:06,240 --> 00:26:08,120
and flees at an incredible speed.

167
00:26:08,120 --> 00:26:10,080
So you can, like, a human being will never

168
00:26:10,080 --> 00:26:12,480
catch a crawfish from the front of the net.

169
00:26:12,480 --> 00:26:15,000
And then it took us about five minutes to say,

170
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:16,960
let's come up behind the crawfish.

171
00:26:16,960 --> 00:26:18,040
And then what will happen?

172
00:26:18,040 --> 00:26:20,320
What happens is they immediately kick themselves,

173
00:26:20,320 --> 00:26:22,920
or like, fling themselves into your net.

174
00:26:22,920 --> 00:26:24,280
And we caught them all.

175
00:26:24,280 --> 00:26:24,840
That was it.

176
00:26:24,840 --> 00:26:26,120
There was no learning.

177
00:26:26,120 --> 00:26:28,280
They weren't saying, hold on, fellow crawfish,

178
00:26:28,280 --> 00:26:30,160
we must revise our strategy.

179
00:26:30,160 --> 00:26:32,120
No, they just did what they were called to do,

180
00:26:32,120 --> 00:26:33,120
and they were all eaten.

181
00:26:36,040 --> 00:26:38,400
OK, so anyway, when you're playing into nature,

182
00:26:38,400 --> 00:26:40,560
you don't worry that your opponent's going to change

183
00:26:40,560 --> 00:26:41,600
his behavior in response.

184
00:26:41,600 --> 00:26:46,280
Certainly, if your opponent is the forest or a mudslide,

185
00:26:46,280 --> 00:26:48,040
you don't think, what will the mudslide do

186
00:26:48,040 --> 00:26:50,160
if I start building dams or something like that?

187
00:26:50,160 --> 00:26:51,600
So you're like, the mudslides are

188
00:26:51,600 --> 00:26:53,320
going to change their behavior.

189
00:26:53,320 --> 00:26:56,360
They're just going to keep doing whatever thing they do.

190
00:26:56,360 --> 00:26:58,960
But on the other hand, when there are two people involved,

191
00:26:58,960 --> 00:27:00,480
then things are very different.

192
00:27:00,480 --> 00:27:02,200
And the idea that a human being might

193
00:27:02,200 --> 00:27:04,960
start revising a strategy in response to your behavior

194
00:27:04,960 --> 00:27:06,240
seems very reasonable.

195
00:27:06,240 --> 00:27:08,640
Again, if a human being is two years old, then maybe not.

196
00:27:08,640 --> 00:27:10,720
Although, it doesn't take kids that long

197
00:27:10,720 --> 00:27:13,200
before they start looking for weaknesses

198
00:27:13,200 --> 00:27:15,440
and sensing chinks in the armor and realizing,

199
00:27:15,440 --> 00:27:19,080
hey, this lady just does whatever we want.

200
00:27:19,080 --> 00:27:21,360
And that guy over there, he's tough.

201
00:27:21,360 --> 00:27:23,200
He's mean.

202
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:25,720
Let's go and exploit the nice lady

203
00:27:25,720 --> 00:27:27,960
and be nice to the mean guy.

204
00:27:27,960 --> 00:27:29,360
Typical thing for kids to do.

205
00:27:32,360 --> 00:27:36,200
All right, so anyway, so given the incredible complexity

206
00:27:36,200 --> 00:27:40,320
of strategic interaction between human beings,

207
00:27:40,320 --> 00:27:42,960
economists have taken a lot of our brain power

208
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:46,640
and zeroed in on a very special case.

209
00:27:46,640 --> 00:27:48,960
Zeroed in on a very special case.

210
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:53,160
And this case is, imagine that we don't have one agent,

211
00:27:53,160 --> 00:27:54,720
but we don't have two agents either.

212
00:27:54,720 --> 00:27:56,720
Instead, we just have a ton of agents,

213
00:27:56,720 --> 00:27:59,920
an enormous ocean of agents.

214
00:27:59,920 --> 00:28:02,920
So let's think about situations like this.

215
00:28:02,920 --> 00:28:05,760
And in this case, we're able to solve the strategy problem

216
00:28:05,760 --> 00:28:09,240
as well, because we said, look, there's that many agents

217
00:28:09,240 --> 00:28:11,160
that no one individual needs to worry

218
00:28:11,160 --> 00:28:13,320
what anyone else's region response to his behavior will

219
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:14,320
be.

220
00:28:14,320 --> 00:28:14,840
Picture this.

221
00:28:14,840 --> 00:28:16,500
Have you ever been to the grocery store

222
00:28:16,500 --> 00:28:18,800
and you're about to buy a second can of corn?

223
00:28:18,800 --> 00:28:20,200
And then you say, wait a second.

224
00:28:20,200 --> 00:28:21,600
If I buy the second can of corn, they

225
00:28:21,600 --> 00:28:23,840
might jack up the price in the first can of corn,

226
00:28:23,840 --> 00:28:25,840
so I better not do it.

227
00:28:25,840 --> 00:28:28,280
Has anyone ever had this kind of reaction?

228
00:28:28,280 --> 00:28:30,880
Now, if you were, say, the US military

229
00:28:30,880 --> 00:28:34,400
and you buy 20 million cans of corn a year,

230
00:28:34,400 --> 00:28:37,480
then this could be a very reasonable concern for you.

231
00:28:37,480 --> 00:28:40,240
But if you're just one isolated individual,

232
00:28:40,240 --> 00:28:42,800
the idea that the store is going to change the price of corn

233
00:28:42,800 --> 00:28:45,680
or that you might tilt the world price of corn by even a penny

234
00:28:45,680 --> 00:28:47,680
sounds quite fanciful, right?

235
00:28:47,680 --> 00:28:49,160
So there are actually a lot of cases

236
00:28:49,160 --> 00:28:52,760
where you will be tailoring your behavior to circumstances

237
00:28:52,760 --> 00:28:55,520
without the slightest concern that anyone else is going

238
00:28:55,520 --> 00:28:57,480
to change their behavior.

239
00:28:57,480 --> 00:28:58,720
Another example.

240
00:28:58,720 --> 00:29:01,080
Is anyone worried that if you drive more aggressively,

241
00:29:01,080 --> 00:29:03,220
this is going to lead to a general social breakdown

242
00:29:03,220 --> 00:29:04,760
of good driving?

243
00:29:04,760 --> 00:29:07,960
Even a small change in the general social level

244
00:29:07,960 --> 00:29:10,480
of good driving if you drive worse.

245
00:29:10,480 --> 00:29:11,880
I once did have a graduate student

246
00:29:11,880 --> 00:29:13,600
who did believe this, and I believe

247
00:29:13,600 --> 00:29:14,680
there's a chain reaction.

248
00:29:14,680 --> 00:29:18,080
And all right, well, I can't prove you're wrong,

249
00:29:18,080 --> 00:29:21,760
but that seems a very strange view about how the world works,

250
00:29:21,760 --> 00:29:23,680
that lots of other people are going

251
00:29:23,680 --> 00:29:26,040
to noticeably change their driving just because you're not

252
00:29:26,040 --> 00:29:27,480
driving that well.

253
00:29:27,480 --> 00:29:29,400
Seems kind of fanciful.

254
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:29,960
All right.

255
00:29:29,960 --> 00:29:32,920
So we have lots of kinds of examples of this thinking.

256
00:29:32,920 --> 00:29:35,280
Has anyone ever been thinking about buying a stock

257
00:29:35,280 --> 00:29:36,080
and you're worried that you're going

258
00:29:36,080 --> 00:29:37,520
to change the world price of stock?

259
00:29:37,520 --> 00:29:39,800
Or what if I dump this?

260
00:29:39,800 --> 00:29:40,560
All right.

261
00:29:40,560 --> 00:29:42,440
So again, I know for all I know, there's

