00:58:48,320 --> 00:58:50,240
increasing in all commodities.

459
00:58:50,240 --> 00:58:53,280
This is actually stronger than necessary.

460
00:58:53,280 --> 00:58:55,400
One of the games that mathematical economists play

461
00:58:55,400 --> 00:58:57,480
is what is the weakest assumption that

462
00:58:57,480 --> 00:59:00,400
guarantees absolute certainty.

463
00:59:00,400 --> 00:59:02,400
And if you're really curious, it turns out

464
00:59:02,400 --> 00:59:04,820
the weakest assumption that guarantees absolute certainty

465
00:59:04,820 --> 00:59:06,760
is called local non-satiation.

466
00:59:06,760 --> 00:59:08,720
If this sounds really boring to you,

467
00:59:08,720 --> 00:59:10,760
then you'll never need to know it again.

468
00:59:10,760 --> 00:59:13,800
But if you're curious, then you can go and Google that.

469
00:59:13,800 --> 00:59:15,240
You can read the Wikipedia article

470
00:59:15,240 --> 00:59:17,400
on local non-satiation.

471
00:59:17,400 --> 00:59:18,360
All right.

472
00:59:18,360 --> 00:59:21,040
Then consumers start with endowment of commodities.

473
00:59:21,040 --> 00:59:22,800
So endowment of consumer i is going

474
00:59:22,800 --> 00:59:27,720
to be indicated by e super i.

475
00:59:27,720 --> 00:59:31,080
And now e is actually a vector.

476
00:59:31,080 --> 00:59:32,440
Remember what is a vector?

477
00:59:32,440 --> 00:59:34,920
It's a bunch of numbers held as a package.

478
00:59:34,920 --> 00:59:43,880
So your endowment vector could be three pigs, two pancakes,

479
00:59:43,880 --> 00:59:54,560
one pound of cheese, and then three tic-tacs.

480
00:59:54,560 --> 00:59:55,400
So that's a vector.

481
00:59:55,400 --> 00:59:58,440
So that is a four-good vector.

482
00:59:58,440 --> 01:00:14,560
So anything strange?


1
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:01,960
So when you put goods in your shopping basket,

2
01:00:01,960 --> 01:00:03,040
you are buying a vector.

3
01:00:03,900 --> 01:00:04,800
Buying a vector of stuff.

4
01:00:04,800 --> 01:00:07,340
You have a comfortable look at the vectors.

5
01:00:07,340 --> 01:00:08,060
All right.

6
01:00:08,560 --> 01:00:09,020
That's a vector.

7
01:00:09,900 --> 01:00:12,680
Okay. And then, of course, we also have a price vector.

8
01:00:13,160 --> 01:00:17,160
Price vector is going to be, you know, is going to list the prices

9
01:00:17,160 --> 01:00:19,080
of one of each of these objects.

10
01:00:19,080 --> 01:00:20,200
All right.

11
01:00:20,200 --> 01:00:21,440
And then, of course, when you're trying to find

12
01:00:21,440 --> 01:00:24,920
out how much does your whole shopping cart cost,

13
01:00:25,600 --> 01:00:27,680
then you are doing vector multiplication.

14
01:00:27,680 --> 01:00:30,900
You're taking, well, the price of, you know, price of pigs times the number of pigs.

15
01:00:30,900 --> 01:00:31,820
What's the price of pancakes?

16
01:00:31,820 --> 01:00:32,480
Number of pancakes.

17
