What's the price of gouda times the amount of gouda times the price

18
01:00:35,620 --> 01:00:38,580
of gouda plus the number of tic tac times the price of tic tacs?

19
01:00:39,060 --> 01:00:42,300
And that equals whatever you have to pay the cash register on.

20
01:00:42,840 --> 01:00:46,420
Right? You're trying to figure out how much money am I going to have to pay?

21
01:00:46,460 --> 01:00:48,760
Take the price of good one times the amount

22
01:00:48,760 --> 01:00:51,480
of good one times the price of good one plus the amount of good two times the price

23
01:00:51,480 --> 01:00:53,460
of good two plus the amount of the good three times the price

24
01:00:53,460 --> 01:00:58,740
good three, and so on, for all K goods that you purchased.

25
01:00:58,740 --> 01:01:02,420
So there's the continuous price vector, which all consumers

26
01:01:02,420 --> 01:01:04,500
treat as exogenous.

27
01:01:04,500 --> 01:01:06,220
Again, that means that every individual

28
01:01:06,220 --> 01:01:10,020
assumes that that price vector is above them and beyond them.

29
01:01:10,020 --> 01:01:14,100
They have no effect on that price vector at all.

30
01:01:14,100 --> 01:01:16,260
And then we can define a general equilibrium

31
01:01:16,260 --> 01:01:19,540
as follows, a situation in which your consumer

