01:06:17,980 --> 01:06:21,140
And the answer is, well, let me give you a hint.

148
01:06:21,140 --> 01:06:22,020
This isn't economics.

149
01:06:22,020 --> 01:06:24,220
Since this is, in fact, PhD economics,

150
01:06:24,220 --> 01:06:26,500
what is the thing that does vary?

151
01:06:26,500 --> 01:06:27,180
What's left?

152
01:06:27,180 --> 01:06:30,780
Where does the flexibility of the world come from?

153
01:06:30,780 --> 01:06:31,280
Prices.

154
01:06:31,280 --> 01:06:32,460
Prices, yes.

155
01:06:32,460 --> 01:06:33,780
Prices can vary.

156
01:06:33,780 --> 01:06:36,700
Prices are what allows society to be consistent and make

157
01:06:36,700 --> 01:06:37,540
sense.

158
01:06:37,540 --> 01:06:41,660
Prices are what reconciles all of the inconsistent

159
01:06:41,660 --> 01:06:44,100
wants of mankind.

160
01:06:44,100 --> 01:06:47,420
It is what reconciles all the endowments, regardless

161
01:06:47,420 --> 01:06:48,980
what the endowments might be.

162
01:06:48,980 --> 01:06:50,860
The prices are what moves around so that even

163
01:06:50,860 --> 01:06:53,780
if endowments change, if the amount of one thing

164
01:06:53,780 --> 01:06:56,820
becomes greater or smaller, if someone actually

165
01:06:56,820 --> 01:06:59,300
drops all their stuff into the ocean.

166
01:06:59,300 --> 01:07:03,180
Prices are what allows the world to continue to be internally

167
01:07:03,180 --> 01:07:06,020
consistent and make sense.

168
01:07:06,020 --> 01:07:08,340
So the price vector P is what is left.

169
01:07:08,340 --> 01:07:12,060
The price vector P is what is around and can still vary.

170
01:07:12,060 --> 01:07:14,500
And that is, again, general regulatory theory.

