what is the social outcome?

174
01:07:21,620 --> 01:07:23,460
Which is a bigger question that is asking,

175
01:07:23,460 --> 01:07:25,860
what does any one individual do?

176
01:07:25,860 --> 01:07:28,260
And again, that kind of begs the question, well,

177
01:07:28,260 --> 01:07:31,860
can all individuals simultaneously be

178
01:07:31,860 --> 01:07:32,500
maximizing?

179
01:07:32,500 --> 01:07:34,100
Is that even possible?

180
01:07:34,100 --> 01:07:37,300
And the answer is, under these assumptions, it's possible.

181
01:07:37,300 --> 01:07:37,800
Questions?

182
01:07:37,800 --> 01:07:39,780
Everyone content?

183
01:07:39,780 --> 01:07:44,340
Everyone still energized and ready to learn this stuff?

184
01:07:44,340 --> 01:07:46,220
OK.

185
01:07:46,220 --> 01:07:49,260
Now, we come to sufficient conditions

186
01:07:49,260 --> 01:07:52,260
for the existence of general equilibrium.

187
01:07:52,260 --> 01:07:54,300
But first, let's back up.

188
01:07:54,300 --> 01:07:57,380
It's very important to remember the difference in logic

189
01:07:57,380 --> 01:08:00,220
between necessary and sufficient conditions.

190
01:08:03,620 --> 01:08:05,340
Necessary and sufficient conditions.

191
01:08:05,340 --> 01:08:07,100
So if something's a necessary condition,

192
01:08:07,100 --> 01:08:09,020
this means that if you don't have it,

193
01:08:09,020 --> 01:08:09,780
you don't have it.

194
01:08:09,780 --> 01:08:11,820
If you lack a necessary condition for x,

195
01:08:11,820 --> 01:08:14,260
you definitely don't have x.

196
01:08:14,260 --> 01:08:15,940
On the other hand, sufficient conditions

197
01:08:15,940 --> 01:08:19,460
tell you if you've got the stuff, you've got x.

198
01:08:19,460 --> 01:08:21,620
But there could be lots of other ways of doing it.

199
01:08:21,620 --> 01:08:23,180
So if you lack a necessary condition,

200
01:08:23,180 --> 01:08:24,900
then you don't have the thing you want.

201
01:08:24,900 --> 01:08:27,900
If you lack a sufficient condition, you might still have it.

202
01:08:27,900 --> 01:08:29,900
Because there could be many different sufficient

203
01:08:29,900 --> 01:08:30,940
conditions.

204
01:08:30,940 --> 01:08:32,740
So you think about this.

205
01:08:32,740 --> 01:08:36,700
So suppose someone has been, yeah,

206
01:08:36,700 --> 01:08:38,340
I kind of go for the gruesome example.

207
01:08:38,340 --> 01:08:40,860
So if I've been decapitated, say, all right,

208
01:08:40,860 --> 01:08:43,060
is that a sufficient condition to kill me?

209
01:08:43,060 --> 01:08:43,740
Yes.

210
01:08:43,740 --> 01:08:45,660
But it's not the only way to kill me.

211
01:08:45,660 --> 01:08:47,020
There's lots of ways to kill me.

212
01:08:47,020 --> 01:08:49,340
You could stone me to death by electrifying me.

213
01:08:49,340 --> 01:08:50,260
There's tons of stuff.

214
01:08:50,260 --> 01:08:52,680
Hopefully, you're not planning on doing any of this stuff.

215
01:08:52,680 --> 01:08:54,580
But there's lots of ways of doing it.

216
01:08:54,580 --> 01:08:55,260
All right.

217
01:08:55,260 --> 01:08:58,860
Now, in general, in mathematics, and especially

218
01:08:58,860 --> 01:09:02,700
in mathematical economics, very often what you're receiving

219
01:09:02,700 --> 01:09:04,180
are sufficient conditions.

220
01:09:04,180 --> 01:09:05,740
And yet, people often be confused

221
01:09:05,740 --> 01:09:07,740
and think they're getting necessary conditions.

222
01:09:07,740 --> 01:09:09,260
So it's very useful to keep in mind,

223
01:09:09,260 --> 01:09:12,100
to remember which thing are you actually giving us.

224
01:09:12,100 --> 01:09:12,700
All right.

225
01:09:12,700 --> 01:09:14,260
So if you had a proof where you said,

226
01:09:14,260 --> 01:09:16,500
look, if you don't have condition alpha,

227
01:09:16,500 --> 01:09:18,220
then it doesn't happen.

228
01:09:18,220 --> 01:09:20,340
Then that's a proof about necessary conditions.

229
01:09:20,340 --> 01:09:21,340
On the other hand, if you said, look,

230
01:09:21,340 --> 01:09:24,300
if these six assumptions are met, then it happens.

231
01:09:24,300 --> 01:09:27,100
Then that's just a proof about sufficient conditions.

232
01:09:27,100 --> 01:09:29,340
And the way you can think about it

233
01:09:29,340 --> 01:09:31,100
is, if you got sufficient conditions,

234
01:09:31,100 --> 01:09:35,140
then you could say, well, so we know.

235
01:09:35,140 --> 01:09:38,500
So if that's happened, then this definitely occurred.

236
01:09:38,500 --> 01:09:40,940
And you say, yeah, but could it still occur in other ways?

237
01:09:40,940 --> 01:09:44,460
Yeah, but then we couldn't know for sure.

238
01:09:44,460 --> 01:09:46,740
So just like in a good zombie movie,

239
01:09:46,740 --> 01:09:49,420
when do you know for sure the zombie's dead?

240
01:09:49,420 --> 01:09:49,920
All right.

241
01:09:49,920 --> 01:09:52,540
Well, decapitation is, again, the standard.

242
01:09:52,540 --> 01:09:53,460
The zombie's fine.

243
01:09:53,460 --> 01:09:55,300
There's nothing that zombie's going to do.

244
01:09:55,300 --> 01:09:57,620
And there's a lot of other cases where it's like, well,

245
01:09:57,620 --> 01:09:59,140
probably he's not coming back.

246
01:09:59,140 --> 01:10:01,180
Probably the zombie's gone.

247
01:10:01,180 --> 01:10:02,900
But again, if you're a mathematician,

248
01:10:02,900 --> 01:10:05,100
you're like, probably not good enough.

249
01:10:05,100 --> 01:10:07,540
If I don't have absolute mathematical proof,

250
01:10:07,540 --> 01:10:11,060
I'm not coming within 100 feet of that zombie.

251
01:10:11,060 --> 01:10:13,420
Zombie movies, that's generally a wise approach.

252
01:10:13,420 --> 01:10:15,060
But anyway, if you're a mathematician,

253
01:10:15,060 --> 01:10:18,660
you are generally obsessed with having absolute certainty.

254
01:10:18,660 --> 01:10:20,940
And that is why they put so much energy

255
01:10:20,940 --> 01:10:23,900
into mathematical proofs that say, we've got absolute proof.

256
01:10:23,900 --> 01:10:25,500
And if all these conditions are met,

257
01:10:25,500 --> 01:10:27,420
then this thing definitely occurs.

258
01:10:27,420 --> 01:10:29,260
So yeah, but maybe it occurred, and maybe we

259
01:10:29,260 --> 01:10:30,260
didn't need that.

260
01:10:30,260 --> 01:10:32,220
What if we just came up with something

261
01:10:32,220 --> 01:10:34,060
that gave us preponderance of the evidence,

262
01:10:34,060 --> 01:10:35,060
and that's good enough?

263
01:10:35,060 --> 01:10:36,860
And we can watch the mathematicians,

264
01:10:36,860 --> 01:10:38,940
the hair on the back of their neck go up,

265
01:10:38,940 --> 01:10:41,420
and the fire in their eyes.

266
01:10:41,420 --> 01:10:44,140
You think that's, what would it be?

267
01:10:44,140 --> 01:10:46,140
Did Ptolemy just say, no, not Ptolemy.

268
01:10:46,140 --> 01:10:47,860
You could just say that he had something

269
01:10:47,860 --> 01:10:50,340
that probably showed about what the angles

270
01:10:50,340 --> 01:10:51,260
of the triangle were.

271
01:10:51,260 --> 01:10:53,220
No, he worked and worked and worked

272
01:10:53,220 --> 01:10:55,300
until he had absolute proof.

273
01:10:55,300 --> 01:10:57,500
And I won't settle for anything less.

274
01:10:57,500 --> 01:11:00,060
That is the mathematician's mentality.

275
01:11:00,060 --> 01:11:02,620
So now we're going to talk about sufficient conditions

276
01:11:02,620 --> 01:11:04,660
for the existence of general equilibrium.

277
01:11:04,660 --> 01:11:08,100
So key point, the equilibrium might exist anyway,

278
01:11:08,100 --> 01:11:10,740
even if these conditions are not met.

279
01:11:10,740 --> 01:11:12,180
And we're going to talk about that.

280
01:11:12,180 --> 01:11:14,140
But if all these assumptions are met,

281
01:11:14,140 --> 01:11:20,180
then we've got a guarantee, a mathematical ironclad proof.

282
01:11:20,180 --> 01:11:22,980
And again, this word proof in that technical mathematician

283
01:11:22,980 --> 01:11:26,100
sense, if this, like, we've proven it,

284
01:11:26,100 --> 01:11:29,500
there is no way in the world this is wrong now.

285
01:11:29,500 --> 01:11:31,060
Unless, of course, the mathematician

286
01:11:31,060 --> 01:11:33,660
made an error somewhere, which could have happened.

287
01:11:33,660 --> 01:11:36,620
But that's not true mathematics.

288
01:11:36,620 --> 01:11:38,700
And you start worrying, what about the fallibility

289
01:11:38,700 --> 01:11:39,620
of the mathematician?

290
01:11:39,620 --> 01:11:41,200
No, no, no, you're putting probability

291
01:11:41,200 --> 01:11:42,900
into a beautiful proof.

292
01:11:42,900 --> 01:11:44,180
It won't let you do it.

293
01:11:44,180 --> 01:11:45,980
No.

294
01:11:45,980 --> 01:11:48,460
So anyways, general equilibrium might still

295
01:11:48,460 --> 01:11:52,020
exist even though sufficient conditions are not met.

296
01:11:52,020 --> 01:11:53,660
Right, all right.

297
01:11:53,660 --> 01:11:55,460
OK.

298
01:11:55,460 --> 01:11:57,500
Yes, and by the way, so like another good example

299
01:11:57,500 --> 01:11:59,040
is you may have ever, how many of you

300
01:11:59,040 --> 01:12:01,420
have ever heard of Arrow's impossibility theorem?

301
01:12:01,420 --> 01:12:01,980
Yes.

302
01:12:01,980 --> 01:12:03,560
If more properly named, it would just

303
01:12:03,560 --> 01:12:06,460
be Arrow's impossible to be certain theorem, which

304
01:12:06,460 --> 01:12:09,540
sounds a lot less cool, right?

305
01:12:09,540 --> 01:12:12,540
Because he doesn't really prove that it's

306
01:12:12,540 --> 01:12:14,540
impossible to meet all of his conditions.

307
01:12:14,540 --> 01:12:17,700
Just proves it's impossible to be certain.

308
01:12:17,700 --> 01:12:19,160
And when you hear that, well, yeah,

309
01:12:19,160 --> 01:12:20,460
I didn't care that much anyway.

310
01:12:20,460 --> 01:12:23,500
So I don't have to be certain as long as it's not that likely.

311
01:12:23,500 --> 01:12:24,980
All right, anyway.

312
01:12:24,980 --> 01:12:28,660
OK, so let's just go through these sufficient conditions.

313
01:12:28,660 --> 01:12:31,300
All right, so first thing to notice,

314
01:12:31,300 --> 01:12:33,860
the inequalities can be replaced with equalities

315
01:12:33,860 --> 01:12:37,940
because we assume strictly increasing utility.

316
01:12:37,940 --> 01:12:42,100
So that's just a little finicky thing that we can take care of.

317
01:12:42,100 --> 01:12:46,060
All right, and then secondly, since this is an endowment

318
01:12:46,060 --> 01:12:49,500
economy, we can multiply all prices through by a scalar

319
01:12:49,500 --> 01:12:51,940
that we'll call lambda.

320
01:12:51,940 --> 01:12:54,980
And this means so if p is an equilibrium price vector,

321
01:12:54,980 --> 01:12:58,540
then lambda times p is as well, where the lambda is a scalar.

322
01:12:58,540 --> 01:13:01,460
So basically, this is like you can knock a 0 off all

323
01:13:01,460 --> 01:13:03,500
of your currency at every price in the economy

324
01:13:03,500 --> 01:13:04,900
and it doesn't change anything.

325
01:13:04,900 --> 01:13:07,180
You've heard about economies where sometimes they just

326
01:13:07,180 --> 01:13:09,180
knock four 0's off the currency.

327
01:13:09,180 --> 01:13:10,820
This is basically that.

328
01:13:10,820 --> 01:13:14,220
It's saying that if we knock four 0's off everything,

329
01:13:14,220 --> 01:13:16,780
then nothing changes.

330
01:13:16,780 --> 01:13:19,020
And what this means is that we can restrict attention

331
01:13:19,020 --> 01:13:22,780
to price vectors where they sum to 1.

332
01:13:22,780 --> 01:13:26,220
You're saying, all right, so OK, but who cares?

333
01:13:26,220 --> 01:13:28,660
Well, the answer is there's a bunch of theorems that only

334
01:13:28,660 --> 01:13:32,620
work if the price vector of a vector sums to 1.

335
01:13:32,620 --> 01:13:34,260
These are big theorems in topology

336
01:13:34,260 --> 01:13:35,900
where that's a key assumption.

337
01:13:35,900 --> 01:13:38,900
And so what this is showing is that we can cleanly

338
01:13:38,900 --> 01:13:44,020
go and take the economy and do a slight trivial transformation

339
01:13:44,020 --> 01:13:46,780
of it, and we can shoehorn it into a theorem that's already

340
01:13:46,780 --> 01:13:50,340
been proven by some really smart people.

341
01:13:50,340 --> 01:13:52,180
And by the way, this is a general strategy

342
01:13:52,180 --> 01:13:55,060
any time you're proving anything in mathematical economics.

343
01:13:55,060 --> 01:13:58,180
Say, is there a theorem that's almost exactly the same

344
01:13:58,180 --> 01:13:59,340
as what I'm trying to prove?

345
01:13:59,340 --> 01:14:00,760
Well, step one, is there a theorem

346
01:14:00,760 --> 01:14:03,140
that's exactly the same as what I'm trying to prove?

347
01:14:03,140 --> 01:14:05,500
In that case, just say, hey, that theorem.

348
01:14:05,500 --> 01:14:06,900
I'm done.

349
01:14:06,900 --> 01:14:08,660
Unfortunately, that won't get you papers.

350
01:14:08,660 --> 01:14:09,300
It's too easy.

351
01:14:09,300 --> 01:14:11,700
So you really want to have some intermediate difficulty

352
01:14:11,700 --> 01:14:14,620
where, say, right, so there isn't any theorem that exactly

353
01:14:14,620 --> 01:14:18,700
fits this, but I can go and do three little transformations,

354
01:14:18,700 --> 01:14:21,900
and I can show that the problem that's economically interesting

355
01:14:21,900 --> 01:14:25,740
is isomorphic to the theorems that are long been known.

356
01:14:25,740 --> 01:14:27,700
But that's not obvious, and so give me tenure

357
01:14:27,700 --> 01:14:29,020
for writing this paper.

358
01:14:29,020 --> 01:14:34,340
OK, so that's important, at least professionally.

359
01:14:34,340 --> 01:14:36,140
So you're familiar with the general way

360
01:14:36,140 --> 01:14:40,860
in which dream jobs for life are handed out in academia?

361
01:14:40,860 --> 01:14:42,460
Are the dream jobs for life handed out

362
01:14:42,460 --> 01:14:43,740
for excellent teaching?

363
01:14:43,740 --> 01:14:44,240
No.

364
01:14:44,240 --> 01:14:44,740
No.

365
01:14:47,220 --> 01:14:51,460
Are they handed out for being a really nice guy?

366
01:14:51,460 --> 01:14:52,140
No.

367
01:14:52,140 --> 01:14:55,460
Once in a little bit, but no.

368
01:14:55,460 --> 01:14:58,420
That will put you over the top.

369
01:14:58,420 --> 01:15:01,060
Are they handed out for publications?

370
01:15:01,060 --> 01:15:02,340
Yes.

371
01:15:02,340 --> 01:15:05,820
What if the publications are ultimately just not that good?

372
01:15:05,820 --> 01:15:07,420
Do people often steal your dream jobs

373
01:15:07,420 --> 01:15:10,220
for life for publications that aren't even that good?

374
01:15:10,220 --> 01:15:12,940
How many publications that weren't that good?

375
01:15:12,940 --> 01:15:14,820
That's an important question.

376
01:15:14,820 --> 01:15:16,940
And of course, this has no bearing

377
01:15:16,940 --> 01:15:19,060
upon our own glorious economics department here,

378
01:15:19,060 --> 01:15:22,380
but I have served on the tenure oversight committee,

379
01:15:22,380 --> 01:15:25,860
and I've seen the horrors of the system up front.

380
01:15:25,860 --> 01:15:30,340
And yeah, so there are a lot of people getting jobs

381
01:15:30,340 --> 01:15:34,620
that I call dream job for life for work that just seems quite

382
01:15:34,620 --> 01:15:36,180
crummy, but they got it.

383
01:15:36,180 --> 01:15:38,260
They racked up a bunch of publications in some place

384
01:15:38,260 --> 01:15:39,260
or other.

385
01:15:39,260 --> 01:15:41,460
Doesn't the quality of the publication matter?

386
01:15:41,460 --> 01:15:43,420
Depends a lot upon the school.

387
01:15:43,420 --> 01:15:45,860
If you're at Harvard, yeah, then in fact,

388
01:15:45,860 --> 01:15:49,540
low-rank publications probably count for negative at Harvard.

389
01:15:49,540 --> 01:15:51,460
But on the other hand, it depends on how low.

390
01:15:51,460 --> 01:15:53,100
But what were you actually able to say?

391
01:15:53,100 --> 01:15:54,860
If you're a assistant professor at Harvard,

392
01:15:54,860 --> 01:15:57,740
then if you want a publication in a low-rank journal,

393
01:15:57,740 --> 01:15:59,900
people say, what in the world is he wasting his time

394
01:15:59,900 --> 01:16:01,420
with those losers for?

395
01:16:01,420 --> 01:16:03,140
So there have been some people at Harvard

396
01:16:03,140 --> 01:16:06,060
who've gotten tenure based upon three papers in the air

397
01:16:06,060 --> 01:16:07,060
or something like that.

398
01:16:07,060 --> 01:16:08,680
Let me have Harvard, go one level down.

399
01:16:08,680 --> 01:16:10,020
Wow, you say.

400
01:16:10,020 --> 01:16:11,060
All right.

401
01:16:11,060 --> 01:16:14,300
But anyway, so where was I?

402
01:16:14,300 --> 01:16:17,340
Yeah, so anyway, but if you just go down

403
01:16:17,340 --> 01:16:19,700
to schools that are ranked, say, 50 to 100,

404
01:16:19,700 --> 01:16:21,940
then it's very common you've got 10 publications some way

405
01:16:21,940 --> 01:16:23,020
or other.

406
01:16:23,020 --> 01:16:24,860
And then all you need to do is combine that

407
01:16:24,860 --> 01:16:27,260
with some letter writers who say that you walk on water,

408
01:16:27,260 --> 01:16:29,660
and that you're awesome, and that your 10 publications are

409
01:16:29,660 --> 01:16:31,060
path-breaking and brilliant.

410
01:16:31,060 --> 01:16:34,220
And how many people cannot find a single human being

411
01:16:34,220 --> 01:16:36,100
willing to write such a letter for them

412
01:16:36,100 --> 01:16:39,020
when their whole future is on the line?

413
01:16:39,020 --> 01:16:40,580
I have yet to encounter a person who

414
01:16:40,580 --> 01:16:44,260
could not get such a letter, just saying.

415
01:16:44,260 --> 01:16:48,060
All right, so this seems like a very cynical view.

416
01:16:48,060 --> 01:16:51,420
I suppose it is, but it doesn't mean it's wrong.

417
01:16:51,420 --> 01:16:53,660
Yeah, so yes, when I was on the committee,

418
01:16:53,660 --> 01:16:55,460
I tried to point out some of these ugly trues

419
01:16:55,460 --> 01:16:57,740
and try to say, perhaps we should raise our standards a

420
01:16:57,740 --> 01:16:58,240
bit.

421
01:16:58,240 --> 01:17:00,660
And generally, I just ran into a brick wall.

422
01:17:00,660 --> 01:17:02,860
Then finally, I found out the best thing

423
01:17:02,860 --> 01:17:04,360
I could do on the committee was just

424
01:17:04,360 --> 01:17:06,500
to stop them from hassling deserving people.

425
01:17:06,500 --> 01:17:08,620
Because sometimes they'll be very deserving people,

426
01:17:08,620 --> 01:17:09,660
like, well, the letter was printed

427
01:17:09,660 --> 01:17:10,820
on official stationery.

428
01:17:10,820 --> 01:17:12,460
I don't think we can count this.

429
01:17:12,460 --> 01:17:15,820
And I was like, come on.

430
01:17:15,820 --> 01:17:18,060
All right, so I felt like I could at least prevent

431
01:17:18,060 --> 01:17:20,020
someone's life from being screwed up.

432
01:17:20,020 --> 01:17:22,020
In the end, of course, they would still get it.

433
01:17:22,020 --> 01:17:25,260
But just to stop someone from screwing someone else,

434
01:17:25,260 --> 01:17:26,780
I can serve that function.

435
01:17:26,780 --> 01:17:28,140
I'm no longer on this committee.

436
01:17:28,140 --> 01:17:31,380
No, it's not that.

437
01:17:31,380 --> 01:17:33,980
It's one of those things where I learned a lot about the world

438
01:17:33,980 --> 01:17:35,380
a little bit worse than I thought.

439
01:17:35,380 --> 01:17:37,180
And I'm traumatized by it.

440
01:17:37,180 --> 01:17:40,700
But still, at least I did look into the belly of the beast

441
01:17:40,700 --> 01:17:42,540
and just see, yeah.

442
01:17:42,540 --> 01:17:44,780
You thought the system was functioned before.

443
01:17:44,780 --> 01:17:46,500
See, and the other thing I'll say,

444
01:17:46,500 --> 01:17:47,940
I know this is a regression advice,

445
01:17:47,940 --> 01:17:49,460
but I do have to mention it, since this does

446
01:17:49,460 --> 01:17:50,780
bear upon your future.

447
01:17:50,780 --> 01:17:53,740
There was one professor with a British accent

448
01:17:53,740 --> 01:17:55,500
who says, a dream job full of life?

449
01:17:55,500 --> 01:17:56,900
What are you bloody talking about?

450
01:17:56,900 --> 01:17:58,140
We work in Fairfax.

451
01:17:58,140 --> 01:18:05,020
And I say, look, it's a job to receive a middle class salary

452
01:18:05,020 --> 01:18:08,540
to do whatever you want without any customers who could ever

453
01:18:08,540 --> 01:18:11,500
really complain about you?

454
01:18:11,500 --> 01:18:13,740
That sounds like a dream job to me.

455
01:18:13,740 --> 01:18:15,740
I think almost every person in the economy

456
01:18:15,740 --> 01:18:17,300
would agree about this.

457
01:18:17,300 --> 01:18:19,060
Of course, if you take that for granted,

458
01:18:19,060 --> 01:18:20,860
it's the worst that could possibly happen.

459
01:18:20,860 --> 01:18:22,780
Then you're good.

460
01:18:22,780 --> 01:18:23,700
All right.

461
01:18:23,700 --> 01:18:25,780
So why don't we go and take a break now?

462
01:18:25,780 --> 01:18:29,100
So that clock is actually three minutes slow.

463
01:18:29,100 --> 01:18:30,540
My apologies.

464
01:18:30,540 --> 01:18:33,180
You will reconvene in 10 minutes.

465
01:18:33,180 --> 01:18:35,540
And again, this is a good time to turn in your homework

466
01:18:35,540 --> 01:18:37,140
if you want to do it physically.

467
01:18:37,140 --> 01:18:39,780
Again, as I said, you can also email directly to the class

468
01:18:39,780 --> 01:18:40,300
today.

469
01:18:40,300 --> 01:18:41,300
Is everyone in the hall?

470
01:18:41,300 --> 01:18:43,380
Or can you just close the door?

471
01:18:43,380 --> 01:18:45,700
When you close the door, if there's people desperately

472
01:18:45,700 --> 01:18:47,500
rushing to get back in, just let them in.

473
01:18:47,500 --> 01:18:49,300
Can you close the door?

474
01:18:49,300 --> 01:18:50,780
All right, great.

475
01:18:50,780 --> 01:18:52,780
OK.

476
01:18:52,780 --> 01:18:54,580
All right, and now, unfortunately, there's

477
01:18:54,580 --> 01:18:56,500
some math we have to do after the break, which

478
01:18:56,500 --> 01:19:00,020
I try to avoid, but can't always be avoided.

479
01:19:00,020 --> 01:19:00,900
All right.

480
01:19:00,900 --> 01:19:05,540
But when possible, I will simply give you the gist of the math

481
01:19:05,540 --> 01:19:09,100
rather than actually working it through in gory detail.

482
01:19:09,100 --> 01:19:11,140
All right, so I've given you all the assumptions.

483
01:19:11,140 --> 01:19:13,940
So we've got the abouts, the conditions that we got.

484
01:19:13,940 --> 01:19:17,420
And now, we then just need the following assumptions

485
01:19:17,420 --> 01:19:20,340
to guarantee the existence of general equilibrium.

486
01:19:20,340 --> 01:19:24,300
All right, so assumption one is the utility

487
01:19:24,300 --> 01:19:26,940
as a function of p has to have a unique solution for all p

488
01:19:26,940 --> 01:19:31,260
for all prices, which, again, may seem like a very odd point.

489
01:19:31,260 --> 01:19:36,380
This is just saying that for any given set of prices,

490
01:19:36,380 --> 01:19:39,180
there's only one utility you're going to get in equilibrium.

491
01:19:39,180 --> 01:19:42,180
You can't have two different utilities that you have.

492
01:19:42,180 --> 01:19:44,300
Yeah, why would you?

493
01:19:44,300 --> 01:19:47,980
All right, assumption two, you need total demand for a good k

494
01:19:47,980 --> 01:19:50,540
exceeds total endowment for a small enough price

495
01:19:50,540 --> 01:19:51,960
and falls short of total endowment

496
01:19:51,960 --> 01:19:53,340
for a large enough price.

497
01:19:53,340 --> 01:19:56,580
So there's got to be a price so high that people actually

498
01:19:56,580 --> 01:19:58,580
consume less than exists.

499
01:19:58,580 --> 01:20:23,580
There's got to be a price so low that people actually


1
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:01,380
want to consume more than exists.

2
01:20:01,380 --> 01:20:02,560
You can get the doubt that they will consume it,

3
01:20:02,560 --> 01:20:04,380
that they want to consume more than exists.

4
01:20:04,380 --> 01:20:07,380
So there's got to be a price so astronomical that people say,

5
01:20:07,380 --> 01:20:10,380
fine, we're going to consume less than actually exists.

6
01:20:10,380 --> 01:20:12,100
There's got to be a price low enough

7
01:20:12,100 --> 01:20:14,420
that people will say we want to consume more than actually

8
01:20:14,420 --> 01:20:15,780
exists.

9
01:20:15,780 --> 01:20:18,260
And then last, we just need the total demand function for k.

10
01:20:18,260 --> 01:20:23,340
Is it got to be continuous for all prices between 0 and 1?

11
01:20:23,340 --> 01:20:26,020
And now, amazingly, we're done.

12
01:20:26,020 --> 01:20:28,540
So how do we actually prove the theorem?

13
01:20:28,540 --> 01:20:30,220
Well, there's the special case.

14
01:20:30,220 --> 01:20:33,460
If it is a two commodity world, then there

15
01:20:33,460 --> 01:20:35,100
is a theorem you may actually remember

16
01:20:35,100 --> 01:20:37,940
from calculus called the intermediate value theorem.

17
01:20:37,940 --> 01:20:40,100
This is basically like a Goldilocks theorem

18
01:20:40,100 --> 01:20:43,180
where it says, look, if sometimes it's too hot

19
01:20:43,180 --> 01:20:44,700
and sometimes it's too cold, there

20
01:20:44,700 --> 01:20:47,980
must be a point at which it is just right.

21
01:20:47,980 --> 01:20:51,140
And that's if there's a continuum of porridge

22
01:20:51,140 --> 01:20:54,940
temperatures, then there must be a bowl of porridge

23
01:20:54,940 --> 01:20:57,020
at a temperature that is exactly right.

24
01:20:57,020 --> 01:20:59,180
So that is the intermediate value theorem.

25
01:20:59,180 --> 01:21:01,740
So if it can be too high, too low, it's continuous,

26
01:21:01,740 --> 01:21:05,260
then you can prove that there must be a point where

27
01:21:05,260 --> 01:21:08,420
you cross whatever threshold that you specifically

28
01:21:08,420 --> 01:21:10,820
are concerned about.

29
01:21:10,820 --> 01:21:13,180
Now, what about the k commodity world,

30
01:21:13,180 --> 01:21:15,220
just an arbitrary number?

31
01:21:15,220 --> 01:21:18,340
Well, here there is something in topology called Brouwer's fixed

32
01:21:18,340 --> 01:21:20,020
point theorem.

33
01:21:20,020 --> 01:21:22,460
Brouwer's fixed point theorem.

34
01:21:22,460 --> 01:21:24,380
And we're not going to go into what it is,

35
01:21:24,380 --> 01:21:26,380
but you invoke this theorem.

36
01:21:26,380 --> 01:21:28,940
And we have actually used all the assumptions

37
01:21:28,940 --> 01:21:33,300
that we need to shoehorn the economy into this famous theorem

38
01:21:33,300 --> 01:21:34,660
in topology.

39
01:21:34,660 --> 01:21:39,500
Now, I tell you, I actually got to watch Gerard Dubrow, who

40
01:21:39,500 --> 01:21:42,020
is, together with Arrow, proved the existence

41
01:21:42,020 --> 01:21:44,500
of general equilibrium using Brouwer's fixed point theorem.

42
01:21:44,500 --> 01:21:46,340
So I actually got to be in a class with him

43
01:21:46,340 --> 01:21:48,820
where he went and did his own proof.

44
01:21:48,820 --> 01:21:51,540
And it went on for 90 minutes.

45
01:21:51,540 --> 01:21:54,260
And he was the famous Nobel Prize winner.

46
01:21:54,260 --> 01:21:55,820
And then after he finished with it,

47
01:21:55,820 --> 01:21:58,820
he was so proud of himself, as proud as only a Frenchman

48
01:21:58,820 --> 01:21:59,700
could be of himself.

49
01:21:59,700 --> 01:22:02,140
And then he finished with it.

50
01:22:02,140 --> 01:22:05,980
And then he finished with it.

51
01:22:05,980 --> 01:22:08,300
And my aunt shot up and said, well,

52
01:22:08,300 --> 01:22:11,060
but didn't you just assume that the economy fits

53
01:22:11,060 --> 01:22:12,700
the assumptions of another theorem

54
01:22:12,700 --> 01:22:13,700
proved by somebody else?

55
01:22:13,700 --> 01:22:15,340
What's so great about that?

56
01:22:15,340 --> 01:22:17,180
Oh, I did something much better than that.

57
01:22:17,180 --> 01:22:30,820
So he never convinced me he had done anything that much better

58
01:22:30,820 --> 01:22:31,340
than that.

59
01:22:31,340 --> 01:22:36,980
And I still stick to my view that that was a bad Nobel.

60
01:22:36,980 --> 01:22:39,580
But obviously, I'm in the minority here.

61
01:22:39,580 --> 01:22:41,900
And a lot of people thought this was freaking brilliant.

62
01:22:41,900 --> 01:22:45,820
So I saw him walk through his own theorem.

63
01:22:45,820 --> 01:22:47,820
And he said, here's Brown's fixed point theorem.

64
01:22:47,820 --> 01:22:48,580
Here's what I did.

65
01:22:48,580 --> 01:22:50,300
And they're like, what you're doing

66
01:22:50,300 --> 01:22:53,060
is just making assumptions until it works.

67
01:22:53,060 --> 01:22:55,100
It's all reverse engineering.

68
01:22:55,100 --> 01:22:57,100
I said, no.

69
01:22:57,100 --> 01:22:59,980
It's much deeper.

70
01:22:59,980 --> 01:23:01,620
All right.

71
01:23:01,620 --> 01:23:04,620
My take is I was right then.

72
01:23:04,620 --> 01:23:05,940
I'm still right now.

73
01:23:05,940 --> 01:23:09,220
And yes, he did something.

74
01:23:09,220 --> 01:23:11,940
But it's not really that great.

75
01:23:11,940 --> 01:23:15,300
It's a sad world where that's worth a Nobel Prize.

76
01:23:15,300 --> 01:23:16,740
Anyway.

77
01:23:16,740 --> 01:23:19,420
But just to give you intuition, so what does Brouwer's fixed

78
01:23:19,420 --> 01:23:20,140
point theorem say?

79
01:23:20,140 --> 01:23:22,180
Or what does any fixed point theorem say?

80
01:23:22,180 --> 01:23:22,780
All right.

81
01:23:22,780 --> 01:23:24,660
So mathematically, the idea is you

82
01:23:24,660 --> 01:23:28,380
want to prove conditions under which there is some f of x

83
01:23:28,380 --> 01:23:30,340
that is equal to x.

84
01:23:30,340 --> 01:23:32,540
Some f of x that is equal to x.

85
01:23:32,540 --> 01:23:34,740
Now intuitively, think about this.

86
01:23:34,740 --> 01:23:36,540
Suppose you look at a globe.

87
01:23:36,540 --> 01:23:43,100
And I were to say, show me where on this globe the globe is.

88
01:23:43,100 --> 01:23:45,340
And you would go and take your little pen and say,

89
01:23:45,340 --> 01:23:46,860
it's right there in Fairfax.

90
01:23:46,860 --> 01:23:50,900
The globe is right there on the globe.

91
01:23:50,900 --> 01:23:51,740
Or it could be a map.

92
01:23:51,740 --> 01:23:56,780
And you could say, all right, where on this map are we,

93
01:23:56,780 --> 01:23:58,740
the people holding the map?

94
01:23:58,740 --> 01:24:00,220
And again, you could say, all right,

95
01:24:00,220 --> 01:24:02,380
we're right there, right there.

96
01:24:02,380 --> 01:24:04,940
Now first, you might say, well, isn't there always

97
01:24:04,940 --> 01:24:06,860
a place on the globe where the globe is,

98
01:24:06,860 --> 01:24:08,480
or a place on the map where the map is?

99
01:24:08,480 --> 01:24:09,460
And the answer is no.

100
01:24:09,460 --> 01:24:11,980
Suppose that we were on the moon holding

101
01:24:11,980 --> 01:24:13,260
the globe of the Earth.

102
01:24:13,260 --> 01:24:16,860
And you say, where on the surface of this globe are we?

103
01:24:16,860 --> 01:24:18,700
We're not there.

104
01:24:18,700 --> 01:24:20,180
We're in a totally different place.

105
01:24:20,180 --> 01:24:23,100
There's no place on this globe of Earth

106
01:24:23,100 --> 01:24:26,380
where we moonwalkers are.

107
01:24:26,380 --> 01:24:28,900
And that is a case where there would not be a fixed point.

108
01:24:28,900 --> 01:24:31,620
So a fixed point would be if you could show where

109
01:24:31,620 --> 01:24:34,740
the map is on the map itself, or where the globe is positioned

110
01:24:34,740 --> 01:24:36,540
on the globe itself.

111
01:24:36,540 --> 01:24:38,060
And the nonexistence of a fixed point

112
01:24:38,060 --> 01:24:41,420
would be one where you are on the surface of the moon

113
01:24:41,420 --> 01:24:42,460
with a globe of Earth.

114
01:24:42,460 --> 01:24:43,740
And you're asked where you are.

115
01:24:43,740 --> 01:24:45,540
And usually you say, it doesn't exist here.

116
01:24:45,540 --> 01:24:47,340
There is no such place here.

117
01:24:47,340 --> 01:24:49,820
And of course, if you are in the United States looking

118
01:24:49,820 --> 01:24:52,300
at a map of Europe, where's America on this map?

119
01:24:52,300 --> 01:24:53,500
America's not on this map.

120
01:24:53,500 --> 01:24:55,300
Not anywhere.

121
01:24:55,300 --> 01:24:58,180
So that is the idea of a fixed point.

122
01:24:58,180 --> 01:24:59,860
Another analogy that's often used

123
01:24:59,860 --> 01:25:03,060
is suppose there's a mountain with a path.

124
01:25:03,060 --> 01:25:05,980
It's winding, but it never actually reverses.

125
01:25:05,980 --> 01:25:08,220
So it always is going down.

126
01:25:08,220 --> 01:25:10,020
Or of course, from the bottom point of view,

127
01:25:10,020 --> 01:25:10,940
it's always going up.

128
01:25:10,940 --> 01:25:12,380
And one person starts at the top.

129
01:25:12,380 --> 01:25:13,940
One person starts at the bottom.

130
01:25:13,940 --> 01:25:16,740
Can we be sure that they will meet each other?

131
01:25:16,740 --> 01:25:18,700
And if we've set it up right, then there

132
01:25:18,700 --> 01:25:21,300
is a fixed point where they will cross for sure.

133
01:25:21,300 --> 01:25:23,140
On the other hand, what if one of them

134
01:25:23,140 --> 01:25:25,340
is skiing down the mountain, and another one

135
01:25:25,340 --> 01:25:27,380
is taking a funicular to the top?

136
01:25:27,380 --> 01:25:29,140
They might never cross.

137
01:25:29,140 --> 01:25:31,020
And again, that would be the case where there

138
01:25:31,020 --> 01:25:34,180
is an absence of a fixed point.

139
01:25:34,180 --> 01:25:36,460
So that's the intuition behind what these fixed point

140
01:25:36,460 --> 01:25:37,700
theorems are all about.

141
01:25:37,700 --> 01:25:39,100
If you're curious, of course, you

142
01:25:39,100 --> 01:25:41,380
can go and read an enormous amount of math

143
01:25:41,380 --> 01:25:42,700
about it for general discussion.

144
01:25:42,700 --> 01:25:43,820
We're not going to do that.

145
01:25:43,820 --> 01:25:46,580
I'll just tell you that at the crucial moment,

146
01:25:46,580 --> 01:25:49,900
Gerard de Brul invoked the Brouwer's fixed point theorem.

147
01:25:49,900 --> 01:25:52,140
And then there's another one that's even more general,

148
01:25:52,140 --> 01:25:54,580
called the Coccatani's fixed point theorem.

149
01:25:54,580 --> 01:25:56,580
If this is all more than you ever want to know,

150
01:25:56,580 --> 01:25:57,300
then well, good.

151
01:25:57,300 --> 01:25:58,860
I'm never going to tell you anymore.

152
01:25:58,860 --> 01:26:01,300
But if you want to learn more, on the other hand, bad news,

153
01:26:01,300 --> 01:26:04,660
because you'll have to pursue this on your own.

154
01:26:04,660 --> 01:26:08,220
OK, so questions about how this works?

155
01:26:08,220 --> 01:26:08,720
Yes?

156
01:26:08,720 --> 01:26:12,820
Unrelated, but sort of related, what do you mean by topology?

157
01:26:12,820 --> 01:26:14,420
Topology is a branch of mathematics

158
01:26:14,420 --> 01:26:17,460
concerned with things like fixed point theorems.

159
01:26:17,460 --> 01:26:21,500
So again, you read the Wikipedia article on what topology is.

160
01:26:21,500 --> 01:26:23,860
Again, honestly, the only topology that I know

161
01:26:23,860 --> 01:26:28,460
is the very topology that I had to learn for this very class.

162
01:26:28,460 --> 01:26:30,100
But basically, it's the mathematics

163
01:26:30,100 --> 01:26:33,180
of shapes and spaces.

164
01:26:33,180 --> 01:26:35,500
Again, I couldn't give you any better description of it

165
01:26:35,500 --> 01:26:38,940
than that, but there is a much more precise story

166
01:26:38,940 --> 01:26:41,540
about what it is.

167
01:26:41,540 --> 01:26:44,660
Now, another question is, how do you get to these fixed points?

168
01:26:44,660 --> 01:26:46,820
So in general equilibrium theory,

169
01:26:46,820 --> 01:26:49,980
they usually focus on Mooraisian auctioneers.

170
01:26:49,980 --> 01:26:52,580
This is the idea that there's just some person who

171
01:26:52,580 --> 01:26:54,940
looks around the world, and any time there's excess demand

172
01:26:54,940 --> 01:26:56,140
for something, he raises its price.

173
01:26:56,140 --> 01:26:58,440
And any time there's excess supply, he lowers its price.

174
01:26:58,440 --> 01:27:00,300
And he does this in a continuous way,

175
01:27:00,300 --> 01:27:02,540
using a very nice blue function.

176
01:27:02,540 --> 01:27:04,340
And in this way, the economy gets

177
01:27:04,340 --> 01:27:06,660
to the equilibrium price vector.

178
01:27:06,660 --> 01:27:08,780
There is a whole literature where Austrians talk

179
01:27:08,780 --> 01:27:13,100
about how terrible this is, and how awful general equilibrium

180
01:27:13,100 --> 01:27:15,900
theory is for dodging this great point.

181
01:27:15,900 --> 01:27:17,700
Because of course, is there actually

182
01:27:17,700 --> 01:27:20,140
an auctioneer in the real world generally?

183
01:27:20,140 --> 01:27:20,900
No.

184
01:27:20,900 --> 01:27:22,660
Sometimes in financial markets, there

185
01:27:22,660 --> 01:27:24,660
will be a market maker who is actually

186
01:27:24,660 --> 01:27:26,580
a lot like a Mooraisian auctioneer.

187
01:27:26,580 --> 01:27:28,060
But then in a lot of other markets,

188
01:27:28,060 --> 01:27:29,580
there's nobody like that.

189
01:27:29,580 --> 01:27:32,340
So this is a bit of a puzzle.

190
01:27:32,340 --> 01:27:34,180
We'll discuss some alternatives later on.

191
01:27:34,180 --> 01:27:35,540
Of course, the really obvious one

192
01:27:35,540 --> 01:27:39,060
is that usually, sellers just set their own price.

193
01:27:39,060 --> 01:27:41,260
And usually, we don't have perfect competition.

194
01:27:41,260 --> 01:27:43,020
So the fact that you set a different price

195
01:27:43,020 --> 01:27:45,180
for a competitor does not mean that you will either

196
01:27:45,180 --> 01:27:47,980
sell an infinite amount or zero.

197
01:27:47,980 --> 01:27:50,480
Like, have you ever noticed that you want to sell something

198
01:27:50,480 --> 01:27:52,940
on, say, eBay, and then you charge a price a little bit

199
01:27:52,940 --> 01:27:56,860
higher than other people, and you still sell it?

200
01:27:56,860 --> 01:27:59,260
In perfect competition, that would never happen.

201
01:27:59,260 --> 01:28:00,740
Or have you ever noticed that you

202
01:28:00,740 --> 01:28:01,980
say I'm willing to work for a little bit less

203
01:28:01,980 --> 01:28:03,580
than other people, and then you don't

204
01:28:03,580 --> 01:28:07,420
find that you receive a trillion job offers?

205
01:28:07,420 --> 01:28:09,740
And again, that's not what perfect competition predicts.

206
01:28:09,740 --> 01:28:11,240
Perfect competition says that if you

207
01:28:11,240 --> 01:28:14,860
offer to work for a penny less than equally qualified workers,

208
01:28:14,860 --> 01:28:18,100
everyone in the world will come and try to hire you.

209
01:28:18,100 --> 01:28:20,700
So far, I don't know of anyone, no matter how successful,

210
01:28:20,700 --> 01:28:24,300
has been offered a job by everyone in the world.

211
01:28:24,300 --> 01:28:25,540
OK.

212
01:28:25,540 --> 01:28:28,140
All right, so questions about this?

213
01:28:28,140 --> 01:28:29,380
Happy?

214
01:28:29,380 --> 01:28:30,140
All right.

215
01:28:30,140 --> 01:28:31,300
So now we're going to do something

216
01:28:31,300 --> 01:28:33,020
which I think will actually enhance your understanding

217
01:28:33,020 --> 01:28:34,420
of what we just done.

218
01:28:34,420 --> 01:28:36,220
And this was never done for me.

219
01:28:36,220 --> 01:28:38,900
So I had to wait years before I actually achieved

220
01:28:38,900 --> 01:28:40,340
this higher level of insight.

221
01:28:40,340 --> 01:28:44,660
So when would a general equilibrium not exist?

222
01:28:44,660 --> 01:28:46,100
It's all well and good to go and say

223
01:28:46,100 --> 01:28:49,420
we proved the existence of general equilibrium,

224
01:28:49,420 --> 01:28:50,880
and we have a bunch of assumptions.

225
01:28:50,880 --> 01:28:53,180
But if you don't understand why the assumptions matter,

226
01:28:53,180 --> 01:28:55,700
then I would still say that you're pretty much in the dark

227
01:28:55,700 --> 01:28:57,740
and don't have any intuition for what's going on.

228
01:28:57,740 --> 01:29:01,700
So rather than going into great detail

229
01:29:01,700 --> 01:29:03,380
on what the fixed point theorems are,

230
01:29:03,380 --> 01:29:05,060
I'm going to go and try to explain to you

231
01:29:05,060 --> 01:29:08,900
why the different assumptions actually make a difference.

232
01:29:08,900 --> 01:29:12,980
So here's some counter examples where general equilibrium would

233
01:29:12,980 --> 01:29:14,660
not, in fact, exist.

234
01:29:14,660 --> 01:29:16,060
All right, first big one.

235
01:29:16,060 --> 01:29:17,780
Lexicographic preferences.

236
01:29:17,780 --> 01:29:20,300
So you guys remember hearing about lexicographic preferences

237
01:29:20,300 --> 01:29:21,420
at some point?

238
01:29:21,420 --> 01:29:24,740
So in the dictionary, when you are alphabetizing words,

239
01:29:24,740 --> 01:29:29,860
suppose that there is a word that is like AZZZZZZZZ.

240
01:29:29,860 --> 01:29:30,940
That's a word.

241
01:29:30,940 --> 01:29:33,740
All right, now does the fact that it has a bunch of Z's

242
01:29:33,740 --> 01:29:36,660
mean that it gets moved later in the dictionary

243
01:29:36,660 --> 01:29:42,300
than a word that has, that's like ABBA?

244
01:29:42,300 --> 01:29:44,500
All right, where the average letter in ABBA

245
01:29:44,500 --> 01:29:47,900
is way closer to A than the average letter in A

246
01:29:47,900 --> 01:29:49,780
with 10 Z's on it, right?

247
01:29:49,780 --> 01:29:53,660
Now, in dictionaries, we use a lexicographic order

248
01:29:53,660 --> 01:29:55,740
where we first look at the first letter

249
01:29:55,740 --> 01:29:57,340
and organize everything that way.

250
01:29:57,340 --> 01:29:58,340
Then we look at the second letter

251
01:29:58,340 --> 01:29:59,740
and organize everything that way.

252
01:29:59,740 --> 01:30:01,420
Then we look at the third letter and organize everything

253
01:30:01,420 --> 01:30:02,700
that way, right?

254
01:30:02,700 --> 01:30:06,380
And this way, we proceed with a clear priority.

255
01:30:06,380 --> 01:30:08,260
First letter is absolute priority

256
01:30:08,260 --> 01:30:10,860
unless the first letter of two words is the same.

257
01:30:10,860 --> 01:30:12,300
Then we look at the second letter.

258
01:30:12,300 --> 01:30:13,220
If the second letter is the same,

259
01:30:13,220 --> 01:30:14,100
we look at the third letter.

260
01:30:14,100 --> 01:30:15,980
If the third letter is the same, we look at the fourth letter.

261
01:30:15,980 --> 01:30:18,220
That's the way that we alphabetize things.

262
01:30:18,220 --> 01:30:20,860
Imagine then that someone had preferences for consumption

263
01:30:20,860 --> 01:30:23,660
this way where they said, look, if there's two bundles

264
01:30:23,660 --> 01:30:26,620
and one has more X, I always choose the one with more X.

265
01:30:26,620 --> 01:30:28,460
If they have equal X, then I choose the one

266
01:30:28,460 --> 01:30:29,820
with it that has more Y.

267
01:30:29,820 --> 01:30:31,540
If they have equal X and equal Y,

268
01:30:31,540 --> 01:30:33,700
then I choose the one that has more C, and so on.

269
01:30:33,700 --> 01:30:36,620
That is what lexicographic preferences mean.

270
01:30:36,620 --> 01:30:40,580
All right, now, if someone has lexicographic preferences,

271
01:30:40,580 --> 01:30:42,740
then it is easy to prove, or maybe it

272
01:30:42,740 --> 01:30:45,940
can be proven anyway, that they cannot have any utility

273
01:30:45,940 --> 01:30:46,860
function.

274
01:30:46,860 --> 01:30:48,820
They do not have any kind of utility function.

275
01:30:48,820 --> 01:30:49,700
Why?

276
01:30:49,700 --> 01:30:52,620
Because if you have lexicographic preferences,

277
01:30:52,620 --> 01:30:54,900
there is no amount of Y that can compensate you

278
01:30:54,900 --> 01:30:57,700
for even the slightest amount of X.

279
01:30:57,700 --> 01:30:59,340
And that's the problem.

280
01:30:59,340 --> 01:31:02,860
OK, now, why does this mean that we could no longer be

281
01:31:02,860 --> 01:31:04,740
sure of the shadow equilibrium?

282
01:31:04,740 --> 01:31:09,740
Well, so suppose that all the agents of the economy

283
01:31:09,740 --> 01:31:11,580
had lexicographic preferences.

284
01:31:11,580 --> 01:31:14,420
Then, so we know, like, everyone lexicographically

285
01:31:14,420 --> 01:31:16,460
prefer X to Y.

286
01:31:16,460 --> 01:31:22,220
Well, is it then possible for X to have a price less than 1?

287
01:31:22,220 --> 01:31:25,220
No, because if the price were less than 1,

288
01:31:25,220 --> 01:31:30,260
what would every single person do in order to get more X?

289
01:31:30,260 --> 01:31:32,900
So if the price of X were, like, 0.999,

290
01:31:32,900 --> 01:31:36,540
and the price of Y were 0.001, if you

291
01:31:36,540 --> 01:31:40,500
had lexicographic preferences, would you keep any Y?

292
01:31:40,500 --> 01:31:42,660
It's really cheap, no, but you dump all your Y

293
01:31:42,660 --> 01:31:47,500
to buy X, because you value X, the smallest amount of X,

294
01:31:47,500 --> 01:31:50,820
infinitely more than you value an infinite amount of Y.

295
01:31:50,820 --> 01:31:53,060
All right, so this means that you say, all right, fine.

296
01:31:53,060 --> 01:31:56,660
Then the equilibrium price is going to be the price of X is 1,

297
01:31:56,660 --> 01:31:58,020
the price of Y is 0.

298
01:31:58,020 --> 01:31:59,620
But that's not an equilibrium either,

299
01:31:59,620 --> 01:32:01,860
because we specified that if the amount of X is equal,

300
01:32:01,860 --> 01:32:04,620
people would still like as much Y as possible.

301
01:32:04,620 --> 01:32:07,140
So if the price of X is 1, then people

302
01:32:07,140 --> 01:32:11,060
want an infinite amount of Y. If the price of X is less than 1,

303
01:32:11,060 --> 01:32:14,740
people want 0 Y. And therefore, it

304
01:32:14,740 --> 01:32:17,700
is possible there would be no equilibrium at all.

305
01:32:17,700 --> 01:32:20,200
And that's an example of the failure of general equilibrium.

306
01:32:20,200 --> 01:32:23,260
There's no price where the world makes sense.

307
01:32:23,260 --> 01:32:25,260
And you say, all right, so the price of X is 1.

308
01:32:25,260 --> 01:32:26,660
Well, then it doesn't make sense,

309
01:32:26,660 --> 01:32:29,660
because people demand an infinite amount of Y,

310
01:32:29,660 --> 01:32:31,500
and there isn't an infinite amount of Y.

311
01:32:31,500 --> 01:32:34,220
So fine, let's make the price of X 0.999.

312
01:32:34,220 --> 01:32:36,380
Well, that's not going to make any sense either,

313
01:32:36,380 --> 01:32:41,180
because now people want 0 Y, and there's more than 0 Y.

314
01:32:41,180 --> 01:32:44,020
So no equilibrium.

315
01:32:44,020 --> 01:32:47,500
The economy does not make sense in this situation.

316
01:32:47,500 --> 01:32:48,620
All right, another example.

317
01:32:48,620 --> 01:32:50,900
Suppose there's a discontinuity of any kind.

318
01:32:50,900 --> 01:32:52,220
So suppose the total demand for X

319
01:32:52,220 --> 01:32:56,820
is 90% of the social endowment at the price of 7,

320
01:32:56,820 --> 01:33:00,620
and 10% of the endowment at price of 7 minus epsilon,

321
01:33:00,620 --> 01:33:02,380
infinitesimal amount.

322
01:33:02,380 --> 01:33:03,900
So why would this be?

323
01:33:03,900 --> 01:33:05,460
Well, maybe society is a superstition

324
01:33:05,460 --> 01:33:08,740
centering on the number 0.7.

325
01:33:08,740 --> 01:33:10,660
All right, have you ever been in a building

326
01:33:10,660 --> 01:33:13,100
where there's no floor 13?

327
01:33:13,100 --> 01:33:14,900
All right, so that's weird.

328
01:33:14,900 --> 01:33:16,660
So I was on a cruise ship.

329
01:33:16,660 --> 01:33:18,100
There's no floor 13.

330
01:33:18,100 --> 01:33:19,980
It's not like it's a laundry floor.

331
01:33:19,980 --> 01:33:21,540
It just doesn't exist.

332
01:33:21,540 --> 01:33:24,420
You go on the stairs, and twilight's on.

333
01:33:24,420 --> 01:33:26,740
You go from 14 to 12.

334
01:33:26,740 --> 01:33:28,700
Where'd 13 go?

335
01:33:28,700 --> 01:33:30,060
All right, same idea.

336
01:33:30,060 --> 01:33:32,620
If there was a superstition about the number 0.7,

337
01:33:32,620 --> 01:33:35,340
so that could lead the people in a discontinuous response.

338
01:33:35,340 --> 01:33:37,180
And then again, no general equilibrium.

339
01:33:37,180 --> 01:33:38,420
There's a question over there.

340
01:33:38,420 --> 01:33:39,460
Yes, please.

341
01:33:39,460 --> 01:33:40,920
Can you have a general equilibrium

342
01:33:40,920 --> 01:33:43,220
with the on-chain preferences?

343
01:33:43,220 --> 01:33:44,300
Ah, so let's see.

344
01:33:44,300 --> 01:33:46,340
With the on-chain preferences, let's see.

345
01:33:46,340 --> 01:33:48,020
So let's see.

346
01:33:48,020 --> 01:33:49,620
So again, the on-chain preferences,

347
01:33:49,620 --> 01:33:51,300
that's actually an example.

348
01:33:51,300 --> 01:33:52,460
So let's see.

349
01:33:52,460 --> 01:33:54,740
So that's one where, let's see.

350
01:33:54,740 --> 01:34:01,300
So you would put 0 value on an additional amount of a good,

351
01:34:01,300 --> 01:34:03,300
unless it came packaged with an additional amount

352
01:34:03,300 --> 01:34:04,100
of the other good.

353
01:34:04,100 --> 01:34:06,860
So yes, I think we actually specifically ruled that out.

354
01:34:06,860 --> 01:34:07,380
Let's see.

355
01:34:07,380 --> 01:34:07,880
Yes, yes.

356
01:34:07,880 --> 01:34:12,380
So that would violate that assumption 4 in section 3,

357
01:34:12,380 --> 01:34:15,620
the utility strictly increasing in all commodities.

358
01:34:15,620 --> 01:34:18,300
Now, could a general equilibrium still exist?

359
01:34:18,300 --> 01:34:20,700
The answer is almost certainly absolutely.

360
01:34:20,700 --> 01:34:25,180
But we no longer have absolute proof that it will exist.

361
01:34:25,180 --> 01:34:28,060
And that is, again, the issue mathematically.

362
01:34:28,060 --> 01:34:30,460
So again, all of these are about sufficient conditions.

363
01:34:30,460 --> 01:34:33,940
So essentially, when normally, when any assumptions fail,

364
01:34:33,940 --> 01:34:36,220
you could just go and tweak all the other assumptions

365
01:34:36,220 --> 01:34:39,380
sufficiently so that everything works out nicely.

366
01:34:39,380 --> 01:34:41,900
Not always, but often you can.

367
01:34:41,900 --> 01:34:45,460
So yes, so with the likes of graphic preferences,

368
01:34:45,460 --> 01:34:47,540
here's a nice tweak that'll make the world work.

369
01:34:47,540 --> 01:34:50,500
Let's just assume there's no y.

370
01:34:50,500 --> 01:34:52,460
Then we've got an equilibrium.

371
01:34:52,460 --> 01:34:53,340
The price of x is 1.

372
01:34:53,340 --> 01:34:54,540
Price of y is 0.

373
01:34:54,540 --> 01:34:55,980
Nobody has any y.

374
01:34:55,980 --> 01:34:57,420
So everyone consumes their diamonds.

375
01:34:57,420 --> 01:34:59,020
Everything's great.

376
01:34:59,020 --> 01:35:01,300
So that'll be fine.

377
01:35:01,300 --> 01:35:02,420
All right.

378
01:35:02,420 --> 01:35:02,940
Let's see.

379
01:35:02,940 --> 01:35:04,820
Then there's a more general concept of demand

380
01:35:04,820 --> 01:35:06,740
not being well-behaved at extreme prices.

381
01:35:06,740 --> 01:35:09,320
This could mean that you just have a 0 price for 0 prices

382
01:35:09,320 --> 01:35:10,180
for some goods.

383
01:35:10,180 --> 01:35:13,820
But there's some technical issues I won't get into.

384
01:35:13,820 --> 01:35:14,700
Here's another one.

385
01:35:14,700 --> 01:35:17,180
Suppose that prices themselves are discontinuous.

386
01:35:17,180 --> 01:35:20,660
Suppose that prices must be in one penny units.

387
01:35:20,660 --> 01:35:25,780
Then it could be that the actual market clearing price is, say,

388
01:35:25,780 --> 01:35:31,620
$1.99 and 7 tenths of a cent.

389
01:35:31,620 --> 01:35:33,660
And then either the price is too high or too low.

390
01:35:33,660 --> 01:35:35,860
And in either case, you actually get market clearing.

391
01:35:35,860 --> 01:35:37,580
And there's no general equilibrium.

392
01:35:37,580 --> 01:35:40,540
So you have that problem.

393
01:35:40,540 --> 01:35:42,980
Now, as I said, these standard theorems

394
01:35:42,980 --> 01:35:44,820
give sufficient conditions.

395
01:35:44,820 --> 01:35:48,060
You can still get general equilibrium

396
01:35:48,060 --> 01:35:50,340
even if some of these assumptions are violated.

397
01:35:50,340 --> 01:35:52,060
So here's a fun one.

398
01:35:52,060 --> 01:35:54,500
Suppose you have a linear utility function.

399
01:35:54,500 --> 01:35:55,340
Many of you remember.

400
01:35:55,340 --> 01:35:57,100
Anyone remember back in intermediate micro?

401
01:35:57,100 --> 01:35:58,940
And you're like, why do we have to do utility functions

402
01:35:58,940 --> 01:35:59,820
like that one?

403
01:35:59,820 --> 01:36:02,900
Why can't it just be utilities x plus y?

404
01:36:02,900 --> 01:36:07,220
Why are you torturing us with these low utility functions?

405
01:36:07,220 --> 01:36:12,900
And the answer is that if your utility is equal to x plus y,

406
01:36:12,900 --> 01:36:16,140
then what does everybody want to do?

407
01:36:16,140 --> 01:36:18,740
So if your utility is x plus y, then you

408
01:36:18,740 --> 01:36:21,900
either want to have all x or all y,

409
01:36:21,900 --> 01:36:23,860
except in one special condition.

410
01:36:23,860 --> 01:36:28,300
So what prices would make a person with utility

411
01:36:28,300 --> 01:36:30,860
equals x plus y want to have all x?

412
01:36:30,860 --> 01:36:31,900
px is less than py.

413
01:36:31,900 --> 01:36:33,500
Yeah, px is less than py.

414
01:36:33,500 --> 01:36:34,860
Then dump all your y.

415
01:36:34,860 --> 01:36:35,820
Buy all x.

416
01:36:35,820 --> 01:36:36,940
Utility's higher.

417
01:36:36,940 --> 01:36:38,380
On the other hand, reverse it.

418
01:36:38,380 --> 01:36:40,980
So if x is more expensive than y, then dump all your x.

419
01:36:40,980 --> 01:36:42,020
Buy y.

420
01:36:42,020 --> 01:36:45,340
If the prices are the same, then you're OK.

421
01:36:45,340 --> 01:36:46,460
Prices of x are the same.

422
01:36:46,460 --> 01:36:50,300
Then you're indifferent between any bundle.

423
01:36:50,300 --> 01:36:53,460
So if it turned out that, say, everyone

424
01:36:53,460 --> 01:36:55,740
had a utility function equal to price of x

425
01:36:55,740 --> 01:36:58,100
plus the price of y, then you might still

426
01:36:58,100 --> 01:37:00,180
be able to get equilibrium.

427
01:37:00,180 --> 01:37:05,020
So if the prices are equal, then maybe everyone

428
01:37:05,020 --> 01:37:07,460
is just content to stay with their initial endowment.

429
01:37:07,460 --> 01:37:08,460
Why not?

430
01:37:08,460 --> 01:37:10,220
And then you're fine.

431
01:37:10,220 --> 01:37:12,260
Now, notice, if you think about it more,

432
01:37:12,260 --> 01:37:14,580
what about a world where almost everybody has a utility

433
01:37:14,580 --> 01:37:16,220
function like that, and then some people

434
01:37:16,220 --> 01:37:17,460
have these weird linear ones?

435
01:37:17,460 --> 01:37:19,660
Is that going to be OK?

436
01:37:19,660 --> 01:37:22,980
Almost certainly fine, because what happens?

437
01:37:22,980 --> 01:37:26,220
So 99% of people have those nice ones,

438
01:37:26,220 --> 01:37:28,380
and a log utility function.

439
01:37:28,380 --> 01:37:30,540
Then 1% of people have the linear ones.

440
01:37:30,540 --> 01:37:32,820
Well, if the price of x is greater than the price of y,

441
01:37:32,820 --> 01:37:35,420
what do the people with the linear functions do?

442
01:37:35,420 --> 01:37:37,180
Price of x is greater than the price of y.

443
01:37:37,180 --> 01:37:38,740
What do they all do?

444
01:37:38,740 --> 01:37:39,940
Dump all their x.

445
01:37:39,940 --> 01:37:41,620
And who buys it?

446
01:37:41,620 --> 01:37:42,580
Those people.

447
01:37:42,580 --> 01:37:44,420
And then the price adjusts, and those people

448
01:37:44,420 --> 01:37:46,380
go and adjust things.

449
01:37:46,380 --> 01:37:48,140
But again, this means that we can no longer

450
01:37:48,140 --> 01:37:53,020
have that pure mathematical Pythagorean certainty

451
01:37:53,020 --> 01:37:55,620
that mathematicians are hunting for, because they say,

452
01:37:55,620 --> 01:37:56,980
well, that's pretty good.

453
01:37:56,980 --> 01:38:00,540
So if there's 99% that will work, but 1% that is OK,

454
01:38:00,540 --> 01:38:02,540
but what if it's 47%?

455
01:38:02,540 --> 01:38:04,380
What if it's radical 3?

456
01:38:04,380 --> 01:38:05,380
What if it's that?

457
01:38:05,380 --> 01:38:07,260
You don't have any proof of anything, do you?

458
01:38:07,260 --> 01:38:11,780
It's all just loosey-goosey garbage, complete garbage.

459
01:38:11,780 --> 01:38:14,500
Very like, well, not a problem that's

460
01:38:14,500 --> 01:38:16,220
really that pressing anyway.

461
01:38:16,220 --> 01:38:18,380
So you could go and work it as a homework problem,

462
01:38:18,380 --> 01:38:19,180
and then you could say, well, we've

463
01:38:19,180 --> 01:38:20,940
proven it for this homework problem.

